1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,446
Yale University.

2
00:00:01,446 --> 00:00:07,363
In a rare book library, a small book sits under strict guard, and nobody can read it.

3
00:00:07,363 --> 00:00:13,697
Its pages hold strange plants, women in green baths, and a script that exists nowhere else.

4
00:00:13,697 --> 00:00:16,133
Is it a message from another world?

5
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,339
Online, its writing is often called an alien alphabet.

6
00:00:20,339 --> 00:00:25,947
Others say a lost language, an unbreakable code, or the greatest hoax in history.

7
00:00:25,947 --> 00:00:30,933
For more than a century, the best codebreakers have failed to settle it.

8
00:00:31,399 --> 00:00:37,789
So today we examine the book itself, one request at a time, the way a researcher would.

9
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:40,066
This is the Voynich manuscript.

10
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:42,145
The Voynich manuscript.

11
00:00:42,145 --> 00:00:43,827
Evidence against theory.

12
00:00:43,827 --> 00:00:46,000
Fill in the first request slip.

13
00:00:50,466 --> 00:00:51,279
Request one.

14
00:00:51,279 --> 00:00:52,566
Who held this book?

15
00:00:52,566 --> 00:00:58,866
Every real object has a paper trail, and this one has a longer trail than most people expect.

16
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:02,733
The trail begins in Prague in the sixteen-thirties.

17
00:01:02,733 --> 00:01:12,333
An alchemist named Georg Baresch owned a book he could not read, and it bothered him so much that he wrote to a famous scholar in Rome for help.

18
00:01:12,799 --> 00:01:20,381
That scholar was Athanasius Kircher, a Jesuit with a vast library and a reputation for solving puzzles.

19
00:01:20,381 --> 00:01:23,400
He is not known to have cracked this one.

20
00:01:23,866 --> 00:01:32,880
In the mid sixteen-sixties, Baresch's friend Johannes Marcus Marci sent the book on to Kircher, with a letter that still survives.

21
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:39,466
The letter repeats a story: Emperor Rudolf the Second once paid six hundred gold ducats for it.

22
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:47,474
The emperor, the letter says, believed the author was Roger Bacon, the thirteenth century English friar.

23
00:01:47,474 --> 00:01:51,099
Today, Yale calls the Bacon link pure speculation.

24
00:01:51,566 --> 00:01:52,744
The dates break it.

25
00:01:52,744 --> 00:02:00,433
Bacon died around twelve ninety-two, and you will see in a moment that the parchment of this book is much younger than that.

26
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:10,033
Then the book vanishes into a Jesuit collection near Rome, and stays there for more than two centuries, until a rare book dealer comes looking.

27
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,839
In nineteen-twelve, a Polish-American dealer named Wilfrid Voynich bought it.

28
00:02:15,839 --> 00:02:18,266
His name has been on it ever since.

29
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:27,466
After his death it passed to his wife Ethel, then to her friend Anne Nill, who sold it to a New York dealer in nineteen sixty-one.

30
00:02:27,933 --> 00:02:36,266
In nineteen sixty-nine the dealer gave it to Yale's Beinecke Library, where it is catalogued as Manuscript four-oh-eight.

31
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:45,966
Since then it has left the building only once, for an exhibition on codes and ciphers at the Folger Shakespeare Library in twenty-fourteen.

32
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:48,341
So the first answer is plain.

33
00:02:48,341 --> 00:02:53,801
This is not a relic dropped from the sky, and not a mystery object with no history.

34
00:02:53,801 --> 00:02:58,866
It is a physical book with owners named in letters from the sixteen-thirties.

35
00:02:59,333 --> 00:03:08,599
A book that Europeans were already puzzling over in the days of horse-drawn carts, centuries before anyone said the words flying saucer.

36
00:03:09,066 --> 00:03:09,876
Request two.

37
00:03:09,876 --> 00:03:11,835
What is it made of, and when?

38
00:03:11,835 --> 00:03:14,333
Here, science can overrule any story.

39
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,429
The pages are parchment.

40
00:03:16,429 --> 00:03:20,300
Protein testing in twenty-fourteen showed it is calfskin.

41
00:03:20,766 --> 00:03:29,599
In two thousand nine, the University of Arizona cut tiny samples from four pages and measured their carbon fourteen.

42
00:03:30,066 --> 00:03:33,972
Carbon fourteen decays at a known rate once an animal dies.

43
00:03:33,972 --> 00:03:39,599
Measure what is left, correct it against tree rings, and you get a date for the skin.

44
00:03:40,066 --> 00:03:41,728
All four samples agreed.

45
00:03:41,728 --> 00:03:49,066
With ninety-five percent probability, the animals died between fourteen-oh-four and fourteen thirty-eight.

46
00:03:49,533 --> 00:03:56,500
That is the early fifteenth century, the decades of Joan of Arc, when printing had not yet reached Europe.

47
00:03:56,966 --> 00:03:57,777
One caution.

48
00:03:57,777 --> 00:04:00,752
Carbon dating tells us when the animal died.

49
00:04:00,752 --> 00:04:04,200
It does not tell us when someone wrote on the skin.

50
00:04:04,666 --> 00:04:13,433
Yale also reports chemical analysis of the inks and pigments, and says they fit materials used in fifteenth century manuscripts.

51
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,527
That sinks two stories.

52
00:04:15,527 --> 00:04:21,471
A forgery by Wilfrid Voynich would have needed old blank parchment and matching ink.

53
00:04:21,471 --> 00:04:26,566
And Roger Bacon died more than a century before these animals were born.

54
00:04:27,033 --> 00:04:29,466
Yale's own cataloguers stay cautious.

55
00:04:29,466 --> 00:04:39,199
They place the book in central Europe, late fifteenth or sixteenth century, and scholars still debate how long after the parchment the writing came.

56
00:04:39,666 --> 00:04:42,120
On dates, then, the case is strong.

57
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:46,116
A real European book, from roughly the fifteenth century.

58
00:04:46,116 --> 00:04:48,500
Not ancient, and not otherworldly.

59
00:04:48,966 --> 00:04:50,533
Now, the hands that made it.

60
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:51,974
Request three.

61
00:04:51,974 --> 00:04:52,879
Who wrote it?

62
00:04:52,879 --> 00:04:55,942
For years people pictured one lonely genius.

63
00:04:55,942 --> 00:04:58,100
The handwriting says otherwise.

64
00:04:58,566 --> 00:05:09,435
In twenty-twenty, the medievalist Lisa Fagin Davis studied individual letter shapes: the angle of strokes, how a pen was lifted, how loops were closed.

65
00:05:09,435 --> 00:05:11,666
She found five different hands.

66
00:05:12,133 --> 00:05:21,227
Two of those hands had been spotted decades earlier by the codebreaker Prescott Currier, who labelled them scribe one and scribe two.

67
00:05:21,227 --> 00:05:22,800
Davis added three more.

68
00:05:23,266 --> 00:05:26,626
Five scribes suggests a workshop or a community.

69
00:05:26,626 --> 00:05:30,826
It does not look like one eccentric hoaxer scribbling alone.

70
00:05:30,826 --> 00:05:36,566
A critical paper has questioned the count of five, so the exact number is debated.

71
00:05:37,033 --> 00:05:44,559
Davis also notes that the parchment is soft, the way a book becomes when it has been handled again and again.

72
00:05:44,559 --> 00:05:47,666
Someone used this book like a working manual.

73
00:05:48,133 --> 00:05:52,150
Now a twist, published in October, twenty-twenty-six.

74
00:05:52,150 --> 00:06:00,033
Davis and Colin Layfield argue the book began as loose single folded sheets, each written by one scribe.

75
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:06,533
Then, they say, someone bound the sheets in the wrong order, perhaps without being able to read a word.

76
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:16,266
The clues: pictures that run off one page and reappear on another far away, and a water stain that does not match from page to page.

77
00:06:16,733 --> 00:06:27,399
Layfield's computer analysis of about thirty-five thousand words found that some pages share vocabulary and others do not, which fits that idea.

78
00:06:27,866 --> 00:06:33,651
If they are right, any study that assumed the current page order must be rethought.

79
00:06:33,651 --> 00:06:37,066
Davis is careful: we do not know if it is a code.

80
00:06:37,533 --> 00:06:38,856
No alien hand here.

81
00:06:38,856 --> 00:06:42,966
Just scribes, quills, and a bookbinder who lost the thread.

82
00:06:43,433 --> 00:06:44,263
Request four.

83
00:06:44,263 --> 00:06:45,922
What do the pictures show?

84
00:06:45,922 --> 00:06:50,199
Because we cannot read the words, the drawings are our only window.

85
00:06:50,666 --> 00:06:56,861
First the herbal section, about a hundred and twenty-six pages, each with one or two plants.

86
00:06:56,861 --> 00:07:04,000
It looks like a normal medieval herbal, except almost none of the plants can be identified with certainty.

87
00:07:04,466 --> 00:07:08,016
That has led some to say they grow on another world.

88
00:07:08,016 --> 00:07:10,065
There is no evidence for that.

89
00:07:10,065 --> 00:07:15,800
Medieval herbals were copied by hand, and drawings drifted a little with every copy.

90
00:07:16,266 --> 00:07:27,542
The astronomical section has circular diagrams with suns, moons and stars, and twelve zodiac signs, including two fish for Pisces and a bull for Taurus.

91
00:07:27,542 --> 00:07:31,399
Those are the same signs Europeans used at the time.

92
00:07:31,866 --> 00:07:36,138
In the zodiac rings, thirty women hold labelled stars.

93
00:07:36,138 --> 00:07:37,166
Strange, yes.

94
00:07:37,166 --> 00:07:43,100
But the layout is calendar-like, and the zodiac itself is plainly European.

95
00:07:43,566 --> 00:07:47,838
Some see a galaxy in one circular drawing with curved arms.

96
00:07:47,838 --> 00:07:57,033
In nineteen-twenty-one, William Newbold read exactly that, a spiral galaxy that he said could only be seen through a telescope.

97
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:01,072
In nineteen thirty-one, John Manly showed the flaw.

98
00:08:01,072 --> 00:08:08,566
Newbold's method let letters be rearranged at will until Latin appeared, so it could prove almost anything.

99
00:08:09,033 --> 00:08:14,119
The bathing section shows women in green pools linked by pipes and tubes.

100
00:08:14,119 --> 00:08:18,369
People have read it as plumbing, medicine, or something else.

101
00:08:18,369 --> 00:08:23,176
One twenty-twenty-four paper argued parts concern sex and conception.

102
00:08:23,176 --> 00:08:26,033
That is an interpretation, not a reading.

103
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:35,633
Then jars and roots that look like a pharmacy, and finally pages of text with small stars in the margins, perhaps recipes.

104
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:45,309
Put together, the pictures describe medicine, stars and bathing: ordinary medieval subjects, drawn in an unusual way.

105
00:08:45,309 --> 00:08:46,410
No spacecraft.

106
00:08:46,410 --> 00:08:47,355
No machines.

107
00:08:47,355 --> 00:08:48,299
No visitors.

108
00:08:48,766 --> 00:08:49,593
Request five.

109
00:08:49,593 --> 00:08:50,929
The words themselves.

110
00:08:50,929 --> 00:08:57,100
About thirty-eight thousand of them, built from roughly two dozen symbols, written left to right.

111
00:08:57,566 --> 00:08:59,542
Here is the strangest part.

112
00:08:59,542 --> 00:09:01,811
The text behaves like language.

113
00:09:01,811 --> 00:09:13,299
The most common word appears about twice as often as the second, and three times as often as the third, a pattern called Zipf's law, found in real languages.

114
00:09:13,766 --> 00:09:23,033
In twenty-thirteen, the physicist Marcelo Montemurro found that words cluster by topic, the way keywords do in real books.

115
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:24,870
But it is also odd.

116
00:09:24,870 --> 00:09:32,229
Some words repeat up to five times in a row, and words that differ by one letter appear far too often.

117
00:09:32,229 --> 00:09:39,299
The famous codebreaker Elizebeth Friedman called the statistical hunt doomed to utter frustration.

118
00:09:39,766 --> 00:09:44,318
Since nineteen twenty-one, claim after claim has appeared.

119
00:09:44,318 --> 00:09:50,596
Hebrew anagrams, abbreviated Latin, a lost Near Eastern language, Aztec Nahuatl.

120
00:09:50,596 --> 00:09:53,500
None has been independently verified.

121
00:09:53,966 --> 00:10:04,546
In twenty-nineteen, a researcher announced he had read it in two weeks: a calligraphic proto-Romance language, written by nuns for a queen of Aragon.

122
00:10:04,546 --> 00:10:11,433
Within hours, Lisa Fagin Davis answered that proto-Romance is not a recognised language category.

123
00:10:11,899 --> 00:10:20,600
She called such methods circular and aspirational: start with a guess from a picture, then search dictionaries until some word fits.

124
00:10:21,066 --> 00:10:23,862
The other camp says there is no message.

125
00:10:23,862 --> 00:10:34,766
In two thousand three, Gordon Rugg showed that Voynich-like text can be generated from a table of prefixes, stems and suffixes, read through a paper grille.

126
00:10:35,233 --> 00:10:43,307
But five scribes, calfskin parchment and a word pattern with topic clusters make a pure hoax an expensive and odd project.

127
00:10:43,307 --> 00:10:48,799
One team of linguists notes a much smaller hoax would have served the same purpose.

128
00:10:49,266 --> 00:10:50,752
Time to score the case.

129
00:10:50,752 --> 00:10:53,466
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

130
00:10:53,933 --> 00:10:58,890
Parchment from fourteen-oh-four to fourteen thirty-eight: evidence.

131
00:10:58,890 --> 00:11:03,329
Owners named in letters from the sixteen-thirties: evidence.

132
00:11:03,329 --> 00:11:06,510
About five scribes in one workshop: likely.

133
00:11:06,510 --> 00:11:09,100
Language, cipher or hoax: unsolved.

134
00:11:09,566 --> 00:11:12,483
Any claimed decipherment: not verified.

135
00:11:12,483 --> 00:11:16,147
Plants and stars from another world: no evidence.

136
00:11:16,147 --> 00:11:18,166
Alien authors: no evidence.

137
00:11:18,633 --> 00:11:19,528
The verdict.

138
00:11:19,528 --> 00:11:20,349
Not aliens.

139
00:11:20,349 --> 00:11:29,228
The Voynich manuscript is a real European book, made of calfskin in the early fifteenth century, by about five scribes.

140
00:11:29,228 --> 00:11:31,466
What it says is still unknown.

141
00:11:31,933 --> 00:11:33,587
That is the honest answer.

142
00:11:33,587 --> 00:11:37,533
The mystery is not that nobody on Earth could have written it.

143
00:11:37,533 --> 00:11:41,033
It is that somebody did, and we have not found the key.

144
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:43,096
And there are new keys.

145
00:11:43,096 --> 00:11:50,520
Put the pages back in their original order, and researchers can test whether the text starts to make sense.

146
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:52,533
Davis says it moves the ball.

147
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:02,433
What lingers is a picture of a scribe, six hundred years ago, dipping a quill and writing something that mattered, for readers we cannot imagine.

148
00:12:02,899 --> 00:12:07,929
Next case: tiny gold pendants from Colombia that look like airplanes.

149
00:12:07,929 --> 00:12:10,700
Ancient aircraft, or stylised animals?

150
00:12:11,166 --> 00:12:14,277
What do you think the Voynich scribes were writing?

151
00:12:14,277 --> 00:12:19,399
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
